ent in me  because it was another  sort of route of right that means i'll  be away from the game longer which means  you know because i kept blaming it on  the game on i kept blaming it on  something else and never really owning  it  that it was it was something that was  wrong with me  that i needed to address um  but i mean i'm in a much better spot now  and i'm more  more capable and more told up in in  terms of dealing with those  those feelings and those emotions and  and that's how i am now  and you're speaking out about it  importantly in a game like rugby very  macho alpha male dominated  uh sport how difficult is it to have  those kind  of conversations  yeah it is is tough we we're definitely  getting  much better as a sport talking about  mental health more opening up more being  more accepting  that we do even as our males  even aggressive rugby players that we  are  we can we can we can still have issues  we can still have problems and we can  still talk  um to each other and and be those guys  we can also still have a laugh and we  still  can take the mick out of each other a  side of talking and helping each other  with with different problems that we can  have um  so if i if me speaking out a little bit  more can  can help others in my sport and others  outside of my sport  um feel more comfortable about opening  up  them than great you talk about a couple  of incidents  i wonder what it was that that took you  to the lowest point the point at which  you felt  that was the time to to ask for help  yeah there's a talk in detail in my book  about  um the the particular incident that took  place at home that that was enough  that was enough enough was enough for me  um my lowest point  and uh it was around march time  um in 2019 that  i then ended up speaking to the club  doctor and  getting help and then he put me in touch  with um  a professional and an independent  professional  that that could then start exploring  what was going on and  um yeah um 